1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,862
Welcome to Code Quest, Chapter 1.

2
00:00:03,233 --> 00:00:08,649
Today you will write your first Python
program, and you will learn to work with

3
00:00:08,669 --> 00:00:09,750
an AI assistant.

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:11,385
Meet Asha.

5
00:00:11,405 --> 00:00:15,729
She helps at her family's cafe, the Corner
Cafe.

6
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:20,039
Her mission today is to make the computer
print this receipt.

7
00:00:20,433 --> 00:00:26,205
By the end of this video, you will
understand every line of this program, and

8
00:00:26,225 --> 00:00:29,063
you will be able to build it yourself.

9
00:00:29,433 --> 00:00:34,578
Along the way, an AI assistant will be
confidently wrong.

10
00:00:34,598 --> 00:00:36,753
Your job is to catch it.

11
00:00:37,333 --> 00:00:38,599
Here is the plan.

12
00:00:38,619 --> 00:00:41,471
Each goal is one piece of the mission.

13
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:44,958
Run a Python file and see its output.

14
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:50,143
Explain how Python runs a program, from
top to bottom.

15
00:00:50,533 --> 00:00:53,335
Control spacing with print, sep and end.

16
00:00:53,733 --> 00:00:57,684
Read an error message and find the line
that caused it.

17
00:00:58,067 --> 00:01:04,339
And use an AI assistant at every stage,
safely, while you check its answers.

18
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:09,929
A program is a list of instructions that a
computer follows exactly.

19
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:13,319
The till at Asha's cafe runs one.

20
00:01:13,339 --> 00:01:16,911
So do train timetables and calculators.

21
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:22,367
AI assistants such as ChatGPT and Claude
can write code.

22
00:01:22,387 --> 00:01:24,275
They are often right.

23
00:01:24,295 --> 00:01:28,545
Sometimes they are wrong, and still sound
sure.

24
00:01:28,933 --> 00:01:34,266
So you are the one who runs the code, and
decides if it is correct.

25
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:36,814
Here is the big idea.

26
00:01:36,834 --> 00:01:41,780
A Python program is a text file that ends
in dot p y.

27
00:01:42,167 --> 00:01:47,941
Python, the program called the
interpreter, reads it and carries out each

28
00:01:47,961 --> 00:01:51,831
line in order: top to bottom, one line at
a time.

29
00:01:52,567 --> 00:01:57,587
Print is a function: a named action that
Python already knows.

30
00:01:57,607 --> 00:02:00,921
You call it with its name, then brackets.

31
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:05,030
And text inside quotation marks is called
a string.

32
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:08,468
Think of a recipe card in Asha's kitchen.

33
00:02:08,488 --> 00:02:10,857
The cook follows each step in order.

34
00:02:11,233 --> 00:02:14,067
The comparison stops at one point.

35
00:02:14,087 --> 00:02:16,752
A cook can guess what you meant.

36
00:02:16,772 --> 00:02:17,927
Python cannot.

37
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:22,727
One typing mistake, and Python stops with
an error.

38
00:02:23,467 --> 00:02:25,329
Let's check that idea.

39
00:02:25,349 --> 00:02:27,811
A file has three print lines.

40
00:02:27,831 --> 00:02:30,892
In which order does Python run them?

41
00:02:42,067 --> 00:02:42,229
B.

42
00:02:42,249 --> 00:02:45,787
From top to bottom, one line at a time.

43
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:48,207
Take a short breather.

44
00:02:48,227 --> 00:02:50,439
Here is what you know so far.

45
00:02:50,833 --> 00:02:56,084
Next, Asha installs Python and writes her
very first line.

46
00:03:00,667 --> 00:03:02,757
Step 1: install Python.

47
00:03:02,777 --> 00:03:04,868
Get the latest version.

48
00:03:04,888 --> 00:03:08,906
Any version from 3.12 works for this
course.

49
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:15,733
On Windows, use the Python install manager
from python dot org or the Microsoft

50
00:03:15,753 --> 00:03:16,223
Store.

51
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:21,317
On macOS, download the installer package
from python dot org.

52
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:25,941
On Linux, Python is usually installed
already.

53
00:03:26,667 --> 00:03:29,619
Then open a terminal and check the
version.

54
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,064
On Windows, type python, space, dash dash
version.

55
00:03:36,467 --> 00:03:40,609
On macOS and Linux, type python3 instead.

56
00:03:40,629 --> 00:03:45,481
You should see a line that starts with
Python 3.

57
00:03:46,067 --> 00:03:46,721
Step 2.

58
00:03:46,741 --> 00:03:54,431
Asha opens a code editor, such as Visual
Studio Code or IDLE, and creates a file

59
00:03:54,451 --> 00:03:56,358
called ex 0 1 hello.

60
00:03:56,733 --> 00:04:00,639
Line 1 starts with a hash sign, so it is a
comment.

61
00:04:00,659 --> 00:04:02,024
Python ignores it.

62
00:04:02,044 --> 00:04:04,333
Comments are notes for people.

63
00:04:04,733 --> 00:04:07,836
Line 2 prints a greeting with her name.

64
00:04:08,233 --> 00:04:10,257
Line 3 prints a second line.

65
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,485
Step 3: run it.

66
00:04:12,505 --> 00:04:17,903
In the terminal, go to the folder that
holds the file.

67
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:23,075
On Windows, type python, then the file
name.

68
00:04:23,467 --> 00:04:28,869
On macOS and Linux, type python3, then the
file name.

69
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:32,044
And there it is: two lines.

70
00:04:32,064 --> 00:04:35,330
Each print makes one line of output.

71
00:04:35,733 --> 00:04:38,334
The quotation marks do not appear.

72
00:04:38,354 --> 00:04:41,880
They only mark where the text starts and
ends.

73
00:04:42,467 --> 00:04:43,098
Step 4.

74
00:04:43,118 --> 00:04:47,473
Asha writes the cafe's tea recipe as a
program.

75
00:04:47,493 --> 00:04:49,613
Step 1, boil the water.

76
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,766
Step 2, add the tea leaves.

77
00:04:53,167 --> 00:04:56,165
Step 3, pour the tea into a cup.

78
00:04:56,567 --> 00:04:59,513
You know how Python reads a file.

79
00:04:59,533 --> 00:05:00,681
Use that now.

80
00:05:11,867 --> 00:05:14,738
The same order as the code.

81
00:05:14,758 --> 00:05:17,201
Step 1, step 2, step 3.

82
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:21,247
Swap two lines in your file and run it
again.

83
00:05:21,267 --> 00:05:26,626
The output changes, because Python always
runs from top to bottom.

84
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:27,827
Step 5.

85
00:05:27,847 --> 00:05:33,372
The receipt needs neat spacing, so Asha
learns two settings.

86
00:05:33,392 --> 00:05:38,085
Print can show several values, separated
by commas.

87
00:05:38,467 --> 00:05:41,756
Sep sets what goes between the values.

88
00:05:41,776 --> 00:05:44,195
By default, it is one space.

89
00:05:44,567 --> 00:05:48,445
End sets what goes after the last value.

90
00:05:48,465 --> 00:05:51,271
By default, it is a new line.

91
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:57,762
Line 2 has a calculation, 3 plus 4, with
no quotation marks.

92
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:03,570
Line 3 prints the cafe's opening time, and
sets sep to a colon.

93
00:06:03,967 --> 00:06:08,893
Line 4 sets end to three dots, and line 5
prints the word done.

94
00:06:20,067 --> 00:06:25,724
Line 2 prints 7, because Python calculates
anything without quotation marks.

95
00:06:26,100 --> 00:06:30,805
Line 3 joins the values with colons, so
they look like a time.

96
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:38,610
And line 4 ends with three dots instead of
a new line, so line 5 continues on the

97
00:06:38,630 --> 00:06:42,095
same line: Loading, dot dot dot, done.

98
00:06:42,833 --> 00:06:43,705
Quick check.

99
00:06:43,725 --> 00:06:47,494
Which setting changes what goes between
the values?

100
00:06:58,667 --> 00:06:59,608
B, sep.

101
00:06:59,628 --> 00:07:01,942
Sep goes between.

102
00:07:01,962 --> 00:07:04,002
End goes after.

103
00:07:04,733 --> 00:07:05,486
Breather.

104
00:07:05,506 --> 00:07:07,290
Relax your shoulders.

105
00:07:07,310 --> 00:07:10,382
Look at how much you can already do.

106
00:07:10,767 --> 00:07:14,497
Now Asha has every piece she needs for the
receipt.

107
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:21,896
Remember the receipt from the start?

108
00:07:21,916 --> 00:07:24,086
Now you can read every line.

109
00:07:24,106 --> 00:07:27,605
Lines 2 to 4 print the header, top to
bottom.

110
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,100
Lines 5 and 6 use sep to draw the dots
between each item and its price.

111
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:37,160
Lines 7 and 8 print the item count and the
total.

112
00:07:37,533 --> 00:07:38,558
Asha runs it.

113
00:07:49,733 --> 00:07:53,031
Items 2, and Total U S D 10.

114
00:07:53,051 --> 00:07:57,888
Python worked out 1 plus 1, and 3 plus 7.

115
00:07:58,267 --> 00:07:59,829
The receipt works.

116
00:07:59,849 --> 00:08:02,027
But it has no order code.

117
00:08:02,047 --> 00:08:09,500
Asha wants to add one, and she will use an
AI assistant to help, one stage at a time.

118
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:15,014
Before Asha opens an AI assistant, she
follows three safety habits.

119
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:15,772
One.

120
00:08:15,792 --> 00:08:20,769
Never paste passwords, keys or private
information.

121
00:08:21,167 --> 00:08:21,463
Two.

122
00:08:21,483 --> 00:08:27,464
Run every piece of AI code yourself, and
compare it with what you asked for.

123
00:08:27,867 --> 00:08:28,372
Three.

124
00:08:28,392 --> 00:08:34,060
You are responsible for the code you use,
even if an AI wrote it.

125
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:36,539
Stage 1: plan.

126
00:08:36,559 --> 00:08:43,197
Before any code, Asha asks how to add the
order code.

127
00:08:43,217 --> 00:08:49,102
She says what she wants, and says: no code
yet.

128
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:52,192
The AI answers with four clear steps.

129
00:08:52,212 --> 00:08:54,831
Each one is something she can check.

130
00:08:54,851 --> 00:08:59,375
A plan you understand is easier to check
than code you do not.

131
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:01,501
Stage 2: write.

132
00:09:01,521 --> 00:09:08,608
Before she changes her receipt, Asha makes
sure she understands every line.

133
00:09:08,628 --> 00:09:13,820
She asks the AI to explain her code, not
to rewrite it.

134
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:20,217
The AI explains each group, and shows
where the order code belongs: after line

135
00:09:20,237 --> 00:09:20,372
3.

136
00:09:20,392 --> 00:09:24,319
Asha checks each sentence against what she
learned.

137
00:09:25,067 --> 00:09:26,694
Stage 3: generate.

138
00:09:26,714 --> 00:09:34,014
Now Asha asks for the code line itself:
one line that prints the order code with

139
00:09:34,034 --> 00:09:34,746
hyphens.

140
00:09:35,133 --> 00:09:41,301
The AI gives a line of code, and says it
prints the order code with hyphens.

141
00:09:41,321 --> 00:09:42,522
It sounds sure.

142
00:09:42,900 --> 00:09:44,236
Look at its code.

143
00:09:44,256 --> 00:09:48,222
You learned exactly what this line does in
step 5.

144
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:00,603
Asha runs it.

145
00:10:00,623 --> 00:10:03,426
It prints spaces, not hyphens.

146
00:10:03,446 --> 00:10:07,001
The AI was wrong, and it sounded sure.

147
00:10:07,021 --> 00:10:08,977
Can she find out why?

148
00:10:08,997 --> 00:10:11,612
That is what testing is for.

149
00:10:12,333 --> 00:10:13,506
Stage 4: test.

150
00:10:13,526 --> 00:10:19,468
Asha gives the AI what she expected, and
what the line really printed.

151
00:10:19,867 --> 00:10:26,854
This time the AI finds both differences,
and names the cause: sep is a space by

152
00:10:26,874 --> 00:10:27,563
default.

153
00:10:27,583 --> 00:10:29,869
It adds sep equals hyphen.

154
00:10:30,267 --> 00:10:32,445
Asha does not trust it yet.

155
00:10:32,465 --> 00:10:33,422
She runs it.

156
00:10:33,442 --> 00:10:38,389
Now the order code is correct, so she adds
it to her receipt.

157
00:10:39,133 --> 00:10:40,447
Stage 5: debug.

158
00:10:40,467 --> 00:10:47,557
While adding the order code, Asha types
Print with a capital P on the last line.

159
00:10:47,577 --> 00:10:49,957
Python stops with an error.

160
00:10:49,977 --> 00:10:53,424
She pastes her code and the full error.

161
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:57,998
She asks for the cause first, then the
smallest fix.

162
00:10:58,018 --> 00:11:04,243
The AI names line 11, explains that names
are case-sensitive, and changes one

163
00:11:04,263 --> 00:11:04,811
letter.

164
00:11:05,533 --> 00:11:07,051
Stage 6: improve.

165
00:11:07,071 --> 00:11:09,042
The receipt now works.

166
00:11:09,062 --> 00:11:15,375
Asha asks for at most two readability
suggestions, each with a reason.

167
00:11:15,767 --> 00:11:17,295
Suggestion 1 helps.

168
00:11:17,315 --> 00:11:20,717
Suggestion 2 says the dots do not line up.

169
00:11:21,100 --> 00:11:23,427
Asha checks it herself.

170
00:11:23,447 --> 00:11:30,671
She counts: both price rows are 23
characters, so they already line up.

171
00:11:30,691 --> 00:11:33,017
The AI was wrong again.

172
00:11:33,037 --> 00:11:38,119
She keeps suggestion 1 only, and runs the
receipt.

173
00:11:38,867 --> 00:11:40,567
Here is the result.

174
00:11:40,587 --> 00:11:45,364
A comment that says what the program does,
on line 2.

175
00:11:45,384 --> 00:11:48,080
And the order code, on line 5.

176
00:11:48,467 --> 00:11:54,045
Every stage helped, and at every stage
Asha checked the answer herself.

177
00:11:54,065 --> 00:11:56,332
That is how you work with AI.

178
00:11:57,067 --> 00:11:59,551
Quick check on what you watched.

179
00:11:59,571 --> 00:12:02,134
Your program stops with an error.

180
00:12:02,154 --> 00:12:03,935
Which stage do you use?

181
00:12:15,133 --> 00:12:15,300
C.

182
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:15,861
Debug.

183
00:12:15,881 --> 00:12:21,746
Paste the code and the full error, and ask
for the cause first.

184
00:12:22,467 --> 00:12:23,214
Breather.

185
00:12:23,234 --> 00:12:29,437
You watched Asha use AI at every stage,
and she caught two AI mistakes by

186
00:12:29,457 --> 00:12:30,204
checking.

187
00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:36,629
Next, the four errors every beginner
meets, including the one Asha debugged.

188
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:42,059
Mistake 1.

189
00:12:42,079 --> 00:12:49,088
The closing quotation mark is missing, so
Python cannot see where the text ends.

190
00:12:49,467 --> 00:12:51,593
It reports a syntax error.

191
00:12:51,613 --> 00:12:57,041
That means Python could not read the file,
so no line runs at all.

192
00:12:57,433 --> 00:13:00,842
The fix: add the closing quotation mark.

193
00:13:00,862 --> 00:13:07,614
Remember this rule: every opening mark
needs its closing partner, for quotation

194
00:13:07,634 --> 00:13:09,586
marks and for brackets.

195
00:13:10,333 --> 00:13:14,398
Mistake 2 is the one Asha debugged with
the AI.

196
00:13:14,418 --> 00:13:18,135
Print with a capital P is a different
name.

197
00:13:18,155 --> 00:13:20,917
Here it is in a short test file.

198
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:23,763
Notice that Start printed first.

199
00:13:23,783 --> 00:13:30,282
This error happened while the program was
running, so the lines above it had already

200
00:13:30,302 --> 00:13:30,593
run.

201
00:13:30,967 --> 00:13:36,392
The report names line 3, and the last line
even suggests the fix.

202
00:13:37,133 --> 00:13:37,970
Mistake 3.

203
00:13:37,990 --> 00:13:44,909
Word processors and some chat apps turn
straight quotation marks into curly ones.

204
00:13:44,929 --> 00:13:47,821
Python accepts only straight ones.

205
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:54,711
So when you copy code from a chat, copy it
from the code block, never from formatted

206
00:13:54,731 --> 00:13:55,100
text.

207
00:13:55,833 --> 00:14:00,884
Now you know three errors, and the rule
about closing partners.

208
00:14:00,904 --> 00:14:02,816
Try the fourth yourself.

209
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:15,718
The closing bracket.

210
00:14:15,738 --> 00:14:19,717
Every opening bracket needs a closing
bracket.

211
00:14:20,467 --> 00:14:21,841
Here are all four.

212
00:14:21,861 --> 00:14:26,102
Each row starts with the last line of its
error report.

213
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:31,439
Unterminated string literal: a quotation
mark is missing.

214
00:14:31,833 --> 00:14:37,225
Name is not defined: check the spelling,
such as a capital P.

215
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:41,668
Invalid character: retype the quotation
marks.

216
00:14:42,067 --> 00:14:45,555
Was never closed: add the closing bracket.

217
00:14:45,933 --> 00:14:47,947
So the habit is simple.

218
00:14:47,967 --> 00:14:52,544
Read the last line first, then find the
line number.

219
00:14:53,267 --> 00:14:54,238
Quick check.

220
00:14:54,258 --> 00:14:57,045
An error report has several lines.

221
00:14:57,065 --> 00:14:59,357
Which one do you read first?

222
00:15:10,533 --> 00:15:10,708
B.

223
00:15:10,728 --> 00:15:12,074
The last line.

224
00:15:12,094 --> 00:15:14,708
It names the kind of error.

225
00:15:15,433 --> 00:15:16,845
One more breather.

226
00:15:16,865 --> 00:15:19,867
Every developer sees errors every day.

227
00:15:19,887 --> 00:15:21,616
Now you can read them.

228
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,208
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

229
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:30,259
First.

230
00:15:30,279 --> 00:15:35,208
The cafe wants the order code written
backwards, with slashes.

231
00:15:35,228 --> 00:15:38,560
What would you change in Asha's code line?

232
00:15:49,733 --> 00:15:55,290
Try it in your editor after the video, and
compare the output with the target,

233
00:15:55,310 --> 00:15:56,935
character by character.

234
00:15:57,667 --> 00:15:58,233
Second.

235
00:15:58,253 --> 00:16:02,000
An AI gives you code that runs with no
error.

236
00:16:02,020 --> 00:16:06,102
What two things do you check before you
trust it?

237
00:16:17,300 --> 00:16:18,591
Keep your answer.

238
00:16:18,611 --> 00:16:21,136
You will use it in every chapter.

239
00:16:21,733 --> 00:16:23,385
Let's recap the mission.

240
00:16:23,767 --> 00:16:28,681
Python runs a program from top to bottom,
one line at a time.

241
00:16:29,067 --> 00:16:34,183
Print shows text and numbers, and
calculations without quotation marks are

242
00:16:34,203 --> 00:16:35,362
worked out first.

243
00:16:35,733 --> 00:16:40,334
Sep goes between values, and end goes
after them.

244
00:16:40,733 --> 00:16:43,128
Read the last line of an error first.

245
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:48,809
And use AI at every stage, then run and
check everything yourself.

246
00:16:49,533 --> 00:16:51,323
One last check.

247
00:16:51,343 --> 00:16:56,389
Asha wants a new line: Tea, dash, U S D 3.

248
00:16:56,409 --> 00:16:58,922
Which code prints it?

249
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:10,253
A.

250
00:17:10,273 --> 00:17:13,890
The separator goes between the two values.

251
00:17:14,633 --> 00:17:16,096
Mission complete.

252
00:17:16,116 --> 00:17:19,758
You earned 100 XP and the First Run badge.

253
00:17:21,833 --> 00:17:24,240
Now it is your turn to build.

254
00:17:24,260 --> 00:17:27,086
The practice file has eight tasks.

255
00:17:27,467 --> 00:17:31,789
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

256
00:17:32,167 --> 00:17:35,781
Three apply tasks, worth 20 XP each.

257
00:17:36,167 --> 00:17:39,210
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

258
00:17:39,230 --> 00:17:43,764
In the last one, you check an AI's work,
like Asha did.

259
00:17:44,167 --> 00:17:47,397
Try each task before you open the
solution.

260
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:51,772
Asha typed every price twice on her
receipt.

261
00:17:51,792 --> 00:17:58,494
In Chapter 2, you will fix that with
variables: store a value once, and use it

262
00:17:58,514 --> 00:17:59,442
many times.

263
00:17:59,833 --> 00:18:02,174
Do the warm-up tasks now.

264
00:18:02,194 --> 00:18:04,819
